{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ספיחיה", :translit "s'ficheha", :gloss "its aftergrowth"} {:he "תשטוף ספיחיה עפר ארץ", :translit "tish'tof s'ficheha afar aretz", :gloss "washes away its aftergrowth the dust of earth"} {:he "פורץ אדם כרמו וסותר גדרו", :translit "poreitz adam karmo v'soter g'dero", :gloss "a person breaks through his vineyard and dismantles his fence"} {:he "אבנים שחקו מים תשטוף", :translit "avanim shachaku mayim tish'tof", :gloss "stones he ground waters wash away"} {:he "אבנים שחקו מים תשטוף ספיחיה עפר ארץ", :translit "avanim shachaku mayim tish'tof s'ficheha afar aretz", :gloss "stones he ground waters wash away its aftergrowth the dust of earth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ספיחיה" "ספיחיה"]}], :frame {:text "The Tosefta Shevi'it (ch. 1) teaches that during the sabbatical year a person may breach his vineyard fence and dismantle his wall. Ba'al HaTurim links this halakhah to the juxtaposition of two rare occurrences of the word ספיחיה: Leviticus 25:11 and Job 14:19 (אבנים שחקו מים תשטוף ספיחיה עפר ארץ). By reading the Job verse as a continuation of the halakhic permission, he derives that even if stones fence the field, one grinds them down, and if floodwaters breach it, one does not repair the breach.", :sources ["Tosefta Shevi'it 1:6" "Leviticus 25:11" "Job 14:19" "Isaiah 5:24"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נזיריה", :translit "n'zireha", :gloss "its untrimmed vines"} {:he "זכו נזיריה משלג", :translit "zachu n'zireha mi'shaleg", :gloss "its branches became as snow"} {:he "סחור סחור", :translit "sachor sachor", :gloss "circle around"} {:he "וטמאה שבועים כנדתה", :translit "v'tam'ah shavu'im k'nidatah", :gloss "and she shall be impure two weeks as in her menstruation"} {:he "סביביו צריו היתה ירושלים לנדה ביניהם", :translit "svivav tzareha hay'tah yerushalayim l'nidah b'ineihem", :gloss "round about her adversaries Jerusalem was as a menstruant among them"} {:he "נדה", :translit "nidah", :gloss "menstruant"} {:he "שנה", :translit "shanah", :gloss "year"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["נזיריה" "נזיריה"]} {:kind :other, :note "Acoustic/structural link between סחור סחור (circle round, i.e., keep away) and the nazirite's separation from vines"} {:kind :gematria, :a "נדה", :b "סביביו צריו היתה ירושלים לנדה ביניהם"} {:kind :gematria, :a "וטמאה שבועים כנדתה", :b ""}], :frame {:text "Leviticus 25:11 commands that the vineyard be untrimmed (נזיריה) in the Jubilee year. The root נזר signifies separation, shared with the nazirite who abstains from vines (Numbers 6:3–4). A separate midrashic calculation (found in Yalkut Shim'oni on Leviticus, and echoed in Ba'al HaTurim) holds that Israel violated seventy sabbatical-and-jubilee cycles during the First Temple period, and the seventy Babylonian years of exile balanced the account. Ba'al HaTurim links this to Leviticus 12:5 (וטמאה שבועים כנדתה), reading שבועים as שבעים (seventy) written defective (without ויו): just as the new mother is impure two-sevens, Israel bore the impurity of seventy violated sevens. That verse's final word כנדתה ('as in her menstruation') stamps Israel's condition as nidduk-separation; the same root governs Lamentations 1:17 (סביביו צריו היתה ירושלים לנדה), where Jerusalem is a menstruant-rejected, and the gematria of that full clause equals נדה × the same numeric base, closing the ring.", :sources ["Leviticus 25:11" "Isaiah 18:5" "Numbers 6:3–4" "Leviticus 12:5" "Lamentations 1:17" "Yalkut Shim'oni on Leviticus 25"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ספיחיה" "ספיחיה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ספיחיה", :refs ["Leviticus 25:11" "Job 14:19"], :n 2, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ספיחיה", :refs ["Leviticus 25:11" "Job 14:19"], :n 2, :lens :exact}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["נזיריה" "נזיריה"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "נזיריה", :refs ["Lamentations 4:7"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "נזיריה", :refs ["Lamentations 4:7"], :n 1, :lens :exact}]} 